CAT = "phrLabor"; SEG = "LAB"
# Functional Area 06, Employee and Labor Relations (20%), second half: union and nonunion
# labor law, complaints, investigations, harassment and ADR.
Q = [
("Under Section 7 of the NLRA, a group of nonunion employees who complain together about pay are:",
 "Engaged in protected concerted activity", ["Unprotected, since there is no union yet", "Breaking the duty of loyalty", "Covered only by state law"],
 "Section 7 protects concerted activity for mutual aid or protection whether or not a union is involved. Supervisors and managers are not covered."),
("Which law added union unfair labor practices and let states pass right-to-work laws?",
 "The Taft-Hartley Act", ["The Wagner Act", "The Norris-LaGuardia Act", "The Landrum-Griffin Act"],
 "The Labor Management Relations Act of 1947 (Taft-Hartley) added union ULPs, banned the closed shop and, in Section 14(b), allowed state right-to-work laws."),
("The Landrum-Griffin Act of 1959 is best known for:",
 "A bill of rights for union members", ["Creating the NLRB", "Setting the federal minimum wage", "Banning child labor"],
 "The Labor-Management Reporting and Disclosure Act gave union members rights such as free speech and secret ballots and required union financial reporting."),
("Which law limited federal court injunctions in labor disputes and made yellow-dog contracts unenforceable?",
 "The Norris-LaGuardia Act", ["The Taft-Hartley Act", "The Railway Labor Act of 1926", "The Wagner Act"],
 "The 1932 Norris-LaGuardia Act restricted injunctions against strikes and barred enforcement of contracts in which workers promised not to join a union."),
("To file a petition with the NLRB for a representation election, a union must show interest from at least what share of the unit?",
 "30%", ["10%", "50%", "66%"],
 "A petition needs a showing of interest, usually signed cards, from at least 30% of employees in the proposed unit. The election itself is decided by a majority of votes cast."),
("During a union campaign, which supervisor statement is lawful?",
 "Sharing facts about the company's pay", ["Asking employees how they will vote", "Promising a raise if the union loses", "Hinting the plant may close if it wins"],
 "Supervisors may share facts and opinions. Threats, interrogation, promises of benefits and spying (TIPS) are unfair labor practices."),
("A unionized employee asks for a union representative at an investigatory interview she believes may lead to discipline. These are called:",
 "Weingarten rights", ["Beck rights", "Garrity protections", "Miranda rights"],
 "NLRB v. J. Weingarten (1975) gives union-represented employees the right to a representative at investigatory interviews they reasonably fear may lead to discipline."),
("Under the NLRA, which subject is a mandatory subject of bargaining?",
 "Wages and hours", ["The company's product prices", "Choice of advertising agency", "The union's internal elections"],
 "Wages, hours and other terms and conditions of employment are mandatory subjects. An employer may not change them on its own during bargaining."),
("Union members strike over the employer's unfair labor practices. Compared with an economic strike, these strikers:",
 "Cannot be permanently replaced", ["Lose all reinstatement rights", "May be fired at once", "Must give 90 days' notice"],
 "Unfair labor practice strikers are entitled to reinstatement when they offer to return. Economic strikers may be permanently replaced."),
("A union contract clause requires disputes over its meaning to go to a neutral third party whose decision is final. This is:",
 "Binding arbitration", ["Mediation", "Fact-finding by a neutral", "Conciliation"],
 "In binding arbitration the arbitrator's decision is final. A mediator only helps the parties reach their own agreement."),
("A typical first step in a union grievance procedure is:",
 "A meeting with supervisor and steward", ["Filing a lawsuit in a federal district court", "A strike vote by the members", "Appeal to the NLRB"],
 "Most grievance procedures start informally between the employee, the steward and the supervisor, then move up management and end in arbitration."),
("Under Title VII, quid pro quo harassment occurs when:",
 "A job benefit depends on sexual favors", ["Coworkers tell offensive jokes", "A customer makes a crude remark", "A rumor about her spreads in the office"],
 "Quid pro quo harassment ties a tangible job decision, such as a raise or keeping the job, to submitting to sexual demands, usually by a supervisor."),
("Under the Faragher/Ellerth defense, an employer can avoid liability for supervisor harassment with no tangible action if it shows it took reasonable care and:",
 "The employee unreasonably failed to report", ["The supervisor apologized to her afterward", "The conduct happened only once", "The employee was a new hire"],
 "The employer must show it used reasonable care to prevent and correct harassment, such as a policy and complaint process, and the employee unreasonably did not use it."),
("An employee complains of harassment by her manager. What should HR do first?",
 "Act to stop it and investigate", ["Wait to see if it happens again", "Tell the manager who complained", "Ask her to sort it out with the manager"],
 "Prompt interim steps, such as separating the parties without penalizing the complainant, and a prompt, impartial investigation are expected of the employer."),
("When interviewing witnesses in a workplace investigation, the investigator should:",
 "Ask open questions and keep notes", ["Share what other witnesses said", "Promise each witness total confidentiality", "Decide the outcome in advance"],
 "Open questions draw out facts without leading. Confidentiality can be kept only as far as the investigation allows, so it should not be promised absolutely."),
("An investigation finds the accounts of the two people involved conflict and there are no witnesses. The investigator should:",
 "Weigh credibility and other evidence", ["Close the case with no finding", "Always side with the complainant in doubt", "Always side with the manager"],
 "Investigators judge credibility by consistency, detail, motive and corroborating evidence such as emails or timing, then reach a conclusion on the evidence."),
("Under Muldrow v. City of St. Louis (2024), an employee challenging a transfer under Title VII must show:",
 "Some harm to a term of employment", ["A significant harm to pay", "A loss of at least one promotion", "An injury serious enough to cause firing"],
 "The Supreme Court held that a forced transfer is actionable if it causes some harm to an identifiable term or condition of employment, without a 'significant' harm threshold."),
("Under Ames v. Ohio Department of Youth Services (2025), a plaintiff from a majority group bringing a Title VII claim must meet:",
 "The same standard as any plaintiff", ["A higher evidence standard", "A 'background circumstances' test", "No evidence standard of any kind"],
 "The Supreme Court unanimously rejected the extra 'background circumstances' requirement, holding Title VII sets the same standard for every plaintiff."),
("An ombudsperson in a nonunion workplace mainly:",
 "Helps resolve complaints informally", ["Decides grievances as a judge", "Negotiates the union contract", "Represents the employer in court cases"],
 "An ombudsperson is a neutral, confidential resource who helps employees and managers resolve problems informally and spots trends to report."),
("A peer review panel decides some employee complaints. This form of alternative dispute resolution is valued mainly because it:",
 "Is seen as fair by employees", ["Removes all legal rights", "Is always binding on the courts", "Needs no written rules"],
 "Panels with coworker members are often trusted by employees, resolve disputes faster and more cheaply than litigation, and need clear procedures."),
]
